{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T10:21:24.472406", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Serine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.74\n- Feedback: The complete network is structurally strong: its molecule and reaction nodes form one connected network, identifiers are consistent, and the intermediate formulas match the intended chemistry. The deterministic check establishes that all ten reactions conserve atoms and charge; there are no conservation violations. The reported coefficients should be included explicitly, and coefficients of eight associated with S8 are ordinary bookkeeping rather than evidence of a malformed reaction. Terminal sulfur coproduct labels and omitted catalytic spectator species do not themselves invalidate connectivity or conservation. Chemically, the photoreductive homologation and Strecker backbone is plausible, and DL-serine satisfies the target without enantioenrichment. Confidence is limited by unspecified UV, pH, catalyst speciation, concentrations, thermal residence times, transfer mechanisms, and competition between productive chemistry and degradation. Direct aminonitrile hydrolysis needs an established serine-preserving operating window; hydantoin ring opening and decarbamoylation are less secure under the proposed natural conditions. Sulfite adducts and mixed-reductant carryover require quantitative treatment, not blanket compatibility or incompatibility assumptions. The network appropriately acknowledges its provisional status and these uncertainties. No cited studies were supplied for a reaction-specific literature audit. P1 is a representative top-scoring route, tied with P3, P5, and P7 at the available evidence level. The ten pathways are overlapping alternatives, not independent demonstrations; their number, hub counts, and environment ordering do not increase confidence. The network is a credible candidate, but is not approved because overall confidence is below 0.90.\n- Issues: ['The sequence is chemically coherent and conservation-compliant. Photoreductive homologation followed by Strecker chemistry provides a plausible route to the correctly identified serine aminonitrile. However, copper/cyanide/sulfide speciation and irradiation conditions are unspecified. The principal completion uncertainty is rxn_007: a hydrothermal label alone does not establish sufficiently rapid nitrile hydrolysis, including passage through the amide, before competing decomposition of the precursor or serine.', 'The hydantoin branch is atom- and charge-balanced and consistent with recognizable Bucherer\u2013Bergs-type chemistry. Nevertheless, rxn_008 requires an effective carbonate/ammonia and pH regime, not merely the presence of CO2. The greater uncertainty is productive nonenzymatic ring opening and decarbamoylation in rxn_009\u2013rxn_010 under natural conditions that preserve serine. Hydantoin formation therefore cannot yet be treated as a demonstrated improvement in target recovery.', 'Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreductive homologation provides a plausible, balanced entry to the same Strecker route. Reversible sulfite\u2013aldehyde adducts do not invalidate the network, but their populations and release rates control substrate availability for rxn_003 and rxn_006. Ferrocyanide availability, photochemical operating conditions, and productive direct aminonitrile hydrolysis remain unspecified. The supplied evidence does not establish a clear superiority over the sulfide route.', 'This is a connected, conservation-compliant route with plausible photoredox and Strecker transformations. Carbonyl sequestration by sulfite requires quantitative treatment rather than an assumption of either complete inhibition or harmlessness. Downstream, carbonate trapping and hydantoin processing need explicit conditions. The network does not establish that rxn_009 and rxn_010 proceed efficiently enough in a realistic heated environment to release and retain serine.', 'Externally supplied formaldehyde is explicitly allowed, so its entry does not create a connectivity or precursor-production defect. Cyanohydrin formation, photoreductive conversion to glycolaldehyde, and Strecker formation constitute a coherent route. Formaldehyde availability and the selective copper/sulfide photochemical regime still require specification. As in P1, the decisive unresolved endpoint is productive aminonitrile hydrolysis rather than the choice of carbon feedstock.', 'The formaldehyde-fed sequence is structurally valid and reaches an appropriate hydantoin precursor through plausible chemistry. External formaldehyde supply does not resolve the downstream uncertainties: rxn_008 needs defined formation conditions, while rxn_009 and rxn_010 require demonstrated nonenzymatic hydrolysis and decarbamoylation. The net equations are sound, but environmental labels do not establish rates, selectivity, or serine survival.', \"This formaldehyde-fed route is connected and chemically coherent. Sulfite/ferrocyanide reduction of glycolonitrile is a plausible source of glycolaldehyde, but actual aldehyde availability and reaction selectivity depend on unreported solution conditions and sulfite-adduct equilibria. The final hydrolysis is chemically recognizable but not established for the unspecified hydrothermal setting. Neither external formaldehyde supply nor the pathway's shorter listing independently warrants a higher score.\", 'The proposed intermediates and net transformations form a valid formaldehyde-to-serine route on paper. Sulfite-bound carbonyl reservoirs require explicit treatment when connecting photochemistry to Strecker formation. Hydantoin capture is plausible but condition-dependent, and its subsequent nonenzymatic processing remains the dominant target-achievement limitation. No conservation defect is present; the concerns are kinetics, selectivity, and environmental implementation.', 'The sulfide-to-sulfite sequence is directed correctly and all reactions conserve atoms and charge. Explicit staging appropriately avoids an unsupported one-pot assumption. However, staging alone does not establish how residual copper, sulfide, and sulfur products affect the subsequent ferrocyanide/sulfite photochemistry. The environmental concern is unresolved carryover and substrate preservation, not the number or order of stages. Direct aminonitrile hydrolysis remains the shared endpoint limitation.', 'The sulfite-to-sulfide route is stoichiometrically valid and logically reaches serine aminonitrile. Its staged interpretation is appropriate, but residual ferrocyanide, sulfite, and carbonyl adducts may alter substrate availability and copper speciation in rxn_004. Copper precipitation or complexation must be assessed rather than presumed harmless or fatal. The additional uncertainty concerns the actual chemical interface between stages; rxn_007 remains insufficiently specified for confident target production.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Serine (DL-serine; no enantioenrichment assumed)\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct glycolaldehyde by experimentally motivated cyanide photoreduction and homologation, then install the amino and carboxyl groups through Strecker chemistry and explicitly resolved nitrile-to-amide-to-acid hydrolysis. Ten distinct graph routes combine five feedstock/photochemical entries with two Strecker order-of-addition branches. These are overlapping alternatives, not ten independently demonstrated syntheses; the cyanohydrin-fed branch ultimately accesses the same imine chemistry. Formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde may enter as externally supplied simple feedstocks despite also being internally formed hub intermediates. Sulfide/copper and sulfite/ferrocyanide modules are kept separate: no pathway switches between them. Sulfite-bound aldehydes are represented explicitly rather than assumed fully available. Hydantoin chemistry is excluded because it adds unresolved ring-opening and decarbamoylation bottlenecks. All modeled transformations are anoxic and use surface settings; hydrothermal labels are not used to imply successful hydrolysis. The graph contains 17 molecule nodes and 13 reaction nodes to retain chemically important adducts and the amide intermediate. It is complete for the intended transformations, not an exhaustive side-reaction network or a validated natural synthesis. In particular, a sufficiently acidic, serine-preserving natural hydrolysis window remains unverified. Numerical operating ranges below are proposed screening conditions unless identified as analytical benchmarks, not reported reaction-specific yields or rate constants. Neutral organic formulas represent equilibrating protonation pools; acid-base counterions are omitted, while the explicitly ionic sulfur equations conserve charge.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Octasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Elemental sulfur; terminal coproduct\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"HSO3^-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfite\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Bisulfite; protonation-state representative of dissolved sulfur(IV)\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"S2O6^2-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Dithionate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Dithionate; modeled sulfur oxidation coproduct\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3O4S^-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydroxymethanesulfonate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Formaldehyde-bisulfite adduct\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5O5S^-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1,2-Dihydroxyethane-1-sulfonate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycolaldehyde-bisulfite adduct\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Iminoethan-1-ol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycolaldehyde imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-Dihydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glyceronitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H5NO2/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h3,5-6H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6N2O/c4-1-3(5)2-6/h3,6H,2,5H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H8N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-3-hydroxypropanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Serinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H8N2O2/c4-2(1-6)3(5)7/h2,6H,1,4H2,(H2,5,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-Serine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-supported photoreduction of hydrogen cyanide to formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Photoactive copper(I)-cyanide complexes; dissolved speciation must be verified\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper(I)-cyanide complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 6.8-7.5; total cyanide 10-50 mM; total dissolved sulfide 10-50 mM; total Cu(I) 0.05-0.2 mM; cyanide-to-copper ratio at least 50. Use a 0.5-2 mm illuminated water layer and 240-280 nm radiation, with 254 nm as a laboratory proxy. Screen incident photon fluxes of 10^14-10^15 photons cm^-2 s^-1 and sample over 0.5-12 h rather than prescribing complete conversion. Exclude O2. Measure dissolved copper complexes, CuCN/Cu2S precipitation, sulfur turbidity, cyanide loss and aldehyde formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical electron transfer reduces the nitrile carbon, followed by hydrolysis of the reduced nitrogen-containing intermediate. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. Formal net equation: 8 HCN + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 CH2O + 8 NH3 + S8. S8 represents the eventual zero-valent sulfur pool, not necessarily the initially formed sulfur species.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Copper/cyanide photoredox chemistry and sulfide-supported precursor synthesis are motivated by Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). The stated numerical screen is not asserted to reproduce a published yield. Total added copper does not establish active catalyst concentration: cyanide complexation competes with copper-sulfide precipitation. Polysulfides, thiocyanate formation, HCN volatilization and optical attenuation can reduce productive flux. A usable stream requires measured carbon and sulfur balances; generic addition of a sulfide mineral would not establish this chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Capture of formaldehyde as glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 7.0-8.5; formaldehyde equivalents 1-20 mM; available total cyanide 5-50 mM; residence 0.5-24 h. Conduct capture in a shaded compartment to separate cyanohydrin equilibration from photochemistry. Formaldehyde hydration and, in the sulfite branch, hydroxymethanesulfonate dissociation must be included in substrate accounting. No selective removal of dissolved metal complexes by settling is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the accessible formaldehyde carbonyl and proton transfer gives HOCH2CN. Net equation: CH2O + HCN -> HOCH2CN. The reaction draws from equilibrating hydrated formaldehyde rather than requiring all formaldehyde to be unhydrated.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolonitrile formation is the established carbon-chain-extension entry used in cyanide photoredox networks (Ritson and Sutherland, 2012; Patel et al., 2015). Its plausibility does not imply quantitative capture in dilute natural waters. For sulfite-derived formaldehyde, rxn_005 must supply sufficient dissociation flux; the adduct cannot simply be counted as free aldehyde.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-supported photoreduction of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Photoactive copper(I)-cyanide complexes\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper(I)-cyanide complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: glycolonitrile 1-20 mM; total sulfide 10-50 mM; Cu(I) 0.05-0.2 mM; pH 6.8-7.5. Maintain and measure the cyanide ligand pool rather than assuming the catalyst survives cyanide depletion. Use 240-280 nm light, a 0.5-2 mm optical path and incident flux 10^14-10^15 photons cm^-2 s^-1. Sample over 0.5-12 h and withdraw at the measured glycolaldehyde maximum. Transfer to a shaded, cooler Strecker compartment; do not introduce ferrocyanide/sulfite chemistry.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Two-electron reduction of the nitrile followed by imine hydrolysis converts the nitrile carbon to an aldehyde while retaining the hydroxymethyl group. Formal net equation: 8 HOCH2CN + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 HOCH2CHO + 8 NH3 + S8.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This homologation is central to experimentally motivated cyanosulfidic precursor chemistry (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). Glycolaldehyde accumulation competes with further cyanohydrin formation, reduction, sulfur chemistry and photochemical loss. A measured irradiation endpoint is therefore necessary. Transport must preserve the product and characterize residual copper and sulfide; shading stops irradiation but does not remove dissolved reagents.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreduction of HCN with formaldehyde-bisulfite capture\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ferrocyanide, [Fe(CN)6]^4-, as a photoredox mediator\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ferrocyanide photoredox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: HCN equivalents 5-20 mM; total dissolved sulfur(IV) 50-150 mM; ferrocyanide 0.1-1 mM; pH 7.0-8.0. Determine the HSO3-/SO3^2- distribution at the actual ionic strength. Use an anoxic 0.5-2 mm illuminated layer, 240-280 nm light and incident flux 10^14-10^15 photons cm^-2 s^-1; sample over 0.5-12 h. This is a separate copper-free catchment or experimental stream, not a downstream switch from rxn_001.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photogenerated reducing equivalents reduce HCN, and the resulting formaldehyde is reversibly trapped by hydrogen sulfite. Sulfur-centered radical coupling provides a possible anoxic donor-oxidation closure. Modeled net equation: HCN + 3 HSO3^- + H2O -> HOCH2SO3^- + NH3 + S2O6^2-. Two sulfur(IV) equivalents supply electrons; the third forms the carbonyl adduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Sulfite/ferrocyanide-mediated HCN homologation is supported as a reaction family by Xu et al. (2018). Explicit adduct capture prevents overestimating free formaldehyde. Dithionate is a chemically plausible anoxic sulfite-radical termination product, but its exclusive formation is not established here; this sulfur closure is a model assumption requiring product analysis. Sulfate or other measured sulfur products would require corresponding additional balanced branches. The abundance and photochemical persistence of natural ferrocyanide also remain environmental constraints.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Release of formaldehyde from hydroxymethanesulfonate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed release screen: pH 8.0-9.0, with dilution or continued cyanohydrin trapping used to shift the equilibrium. Measure total sulfur(IV), its protonation distribution, free-plus-hydrated formaldehyde, adduct concentration and dissociation kinetics over 0.5-48 h. Define K_app = [adduct]/([unbound formaldehyde pool][HSO3-]) at the actual temperature and ionic strength. The unbound fraction is 1/(1 + K_app[HSO3-]) under this two-pool approximation. Evaporation is not treated as sulfite removal.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reverse bisulfite addition releases an equilibrating formaldehyde/hydrate pool. Net equation: HOCH2SO3^- -> CH2O + HSO3^-. This node shows the productive direction of a reversible equilibrium, coupled to formaldehyde consumption in rxn_002.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reversible carbonyl trapping is relevant to sulfite-based homologation chemistry (Xu et al., 2018), but that reaction-family citation does not supply a release constant for this proposed matrix. Productive connection requires the measured adduct-release flux to sustain glycolonitrile formation within the residence time. Dilution lowers both inhibitor and substrate concentrations and is not a free concentration mechanism. The proposed environmental release operation is conditional, not demonstrated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreduction of glycolonitrile with glycolaldehyde-bisulfite capture\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ferrocyanide, [Fe(CN)6]^4-, as a photoredox mediator\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ferrocyanide photoredox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: glycolonitrile 1-20 mM; total sulfur(IV) 50-150 mM; ferrocyanide 0.1-1 mM; pH 7.0-8.0. Use the irradiation geometry and flux range of rxn_004, with a separately measured absorption profile after the first stage. Sample over 0.5-12 h. Account for accumulated dithionate, ferro/ferricyanide speciation and aldehyde adducts. No copper/sulfide stream is introduced.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoreduction and hydrolysis of the nitrile generate glycolaldehyde, followed by reversible bisulfite capture. Modeled net equation: HOCH2CN + 3 HSO3^- + H2O -> HOCH2CH(OH)SO3^- + NH3 + S2O6^2-.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemical homologation supplies a supported alternative to the copper/sulfide module (Xu et al., 2018). The adduct is a reservoir, not automatically either a dead-end sink or freely available substrate. As in rxn_004, the dithionate-only electron-donor closure is provisional and must be checked experimentally. Productive selectivity, catalyst persistence and natural actinic flux cannot be inferred solely from the balanced equation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Release of glycolaldehyde from its bisulfite adduct\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed release screen: pH 8.3-9.2, shaded conditions, 0.5-48 h observation. Quantify adduct, unbound glycolaldehyde including its hydrate pool, and dissolved sulfur(IV). Measure K_app and the adduct dissociation rate in the actual salt matrix. Under a two-pool equilibrium, K_app[HSO3-] <= 1 gives at least 50% sulfite-unbound glycolaldehyde; if this criterion is not met, independently demonstrate adequate release flux into the consuming Strecker reaction. Hydration and oligomerization require additional corrections.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible carbon-sulfur bond cleavage regenerates glycolaldehyde and hydrogen sulfite. Net equation: HOCH2CH(OH)SO3^- -> HOCH2CHO + HSO3^-. Subsequent imine or cyanohydrin formation can pull the equilibrium without requiring instantaneous complete dissociation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This explicit gate addresses the substrate-availability limitation of sulfite photochemistry (Xu et al., 2018). No numerical equilibrium constant or rate is invented. The natural connection remains speculative until those measurements establish adequate flux. Raising pH may facilitate release but also accelerates competing glycolaldehyde chemistry; dilution can improve the unbound fraction while making downstream encounters slower. Sulfite and ferrocyanide carryover must be tested rather than assumed compatible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formation of glycolaldehyde imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.3-9.2; unbound glycolaldehyde equivalents 1-20 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 50-200 mM. Calculate free NH3 using temperature- and ionic-strength-corrected acid-base equilibria. Use shaded water and couple promptly to rxn_009; do not require isolation of an imine from bulk water. Screen 0.5-24 h while measuring carbonyl depletion, aminonitrile accumulation and oligomeric losses. Analyze residual copper, sulfur(IV), sulfide and iron-cyanide species in the appropriate upstream matrix.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia addition forms a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to HOCH2CH=NH. Net equation: HOCH2CHO + NH3 -> HOCH2CH=NH + H2O. The imine may remain at low steady-state abundance and be consumed through its protonated iminium form.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imine formation followed by cyanide addition is the classical Strecker mechanism (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957), applied to the glycolaldehyde precursor pool considered in cyanosulfidic chemistry (Patel et al., 2015). The proposed concentrations are screening values, not a claimed natural inventory. Hydration, ammonia protonation and competing carbonyl reactions prevent assuming quantitative imine formation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide addition to glycolaldehyde imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.3-9.2; available total cyanide 5-50 mM; ammonia-rich conditions inherited from rxn_008. Correct cyanide availability for metal complexation, volatilization and sulfur reactions. Use shaded conditions and follow aminonitrile formation over 0.5-24 h. Transfer the aminonitrile-containing stream promptly to a separately evaluated hydrolysis setting; do not assume storage stability or quantitative recovery during transport.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon, and proton transfer gives HOCH2CH(NH2)CN. Net equation: HOCH2CH=NH + HCN -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CN. Attack on either face produces a racemic aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the chemically direct Strecker route to the correctly connected serine aminonitrile (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957; Patel et al., 2015). It installs the future carboxyl carbon without requiring selective oxidation of a polyfunctional precursor. Retro-Strecker chemistry, oligomerization and cyanide sequestration still require measurement. Formation of this precursor alone is not evidence that the serine endpoint has been achieved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-first capture of glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 6.8-8.0; unbound glycolaldehyde equivalents 1-20 mM; total available cyanide 5-50 mM; initially low free ammonia. Screen 0.5-24 h in shaded water. In sulfite-derived mixtures, quantify simultaneous bisulfite-adduct and cyanohydrin populations instead of treating all total glycolaldehyde as reactive carbonyl.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to glycolaldehyde produces HOCH2CH(OH)CN. Net equation: HOCH2CHO + HCN -> HOCH2CH(OH)CN. Addition to either carbonyl face gives racemic glyceronitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aldehyde cyanohydrin formation is established chemistry and is integral to the homologation systems discussed by Ritson and Sutherland (2012) and Patel et al. (2015). This branch represents a chemically meaningful cyanide-first reservoir when ammonia arrives later. It is not asserted to outperform imine-first Strecker chemistry, and direct hydrolysis of this hydroxynitrile would give glyceric acid rather than serine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of glyceronitrile to serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: glyceronitrile 1-20 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 50-200 mM; pH 8.3-9.2; shaded, anoxic conditions. Follow cyanohydrin, aldehyde and aminonitrile concentrations over 1-48 h. Retain a measurable cyanide pool, including HCN released by cyanohydrin dissociation. Reject the route if hydrolysis, polymerization or carbonyl loss dominates before aminonitrile accumulation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"This is a net reservoir-exchange reaction, not direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group by ammonia. Glyceronitrile dissociates reversibly to glycolaldehyde and HCN; ammonia forms the imine, which is captured by cyanide through the chemistry of rxn_008 and rxn_009. Net equation: HOCH2CH(OH)CN + NH3 -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CN + H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The net conversion follows reversible cyanohydrin and Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Its operation with this substrate under the stated dilute, saline conditions is a conditional extrapolation, not a reaction-specific demonstrated natural synthesis. This branch is included because cyanide-first delivery can populate a real reservoir, but it is not mechanistically independent of the imine-first branch. Reservoir exchange and serine-aminonitrile yield must be measured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of serine aminonitrile to serinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanogenic chloride brine\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"363-370 K; proposed natural-window screen\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Br\u00f8nsted acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed, unvalidated screen: 1-3 M analytical HCl in a concentrated volcanogenic brine; measure proton activity rather than equating it to concentration. Sample at 1, 3, 6, 12, 24 and 48 h. Determine aminonitrile disappearance, serinamide formation, serine formation and identified degradation products independently. Use anoxic, shaded conditions. Measure upstream carryover and run matrix-matched controls; neither settling nor dilution is assumed to selectively remove dissolved copper or iron-cyanide complexes. The occurrence and persistence of such concentrated natural acid are unresolved.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation activates the nitrile carbon toward water addition. Proton transfers and tautomerization give the primary amide. Net equation: HOCH2CH(NH2)CN + H2O -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CONH2. The amino group is predominantly protonated in the reaction medium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acidic conversion of aminonitriles to amino acids is established at the reaction-family level (Miller, 1957), but this does not establish a serine-preserving environmental window for this substrate. This particular operating window is explicitly speculative and is a key completion bottleneck. Resolving the amide prevents a fast hydration step from being mistaken for complete carboxyl formation. A coupled kinetic model must fit aminonitrile-to-amide, amide-to-serine and loss reactions; precursor disappearance alone is insufficient.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of serinamide to serine with explicit survival assessment\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanogenic chloride brine\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"363-370 K; proposed natural-window screen\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Br\u00f8nsted acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: 1-6 M analytical HCl, with separate 6, 12, 24, 48 and 96 h samples rather than an assumed residence time. Quantify serine directly against an authentic standard and expose authentic serine to the same brine to measure loss. Cool withdrawn product streams to 300-310 K. The familiar 6 M HCl, 383 K, approximately 24 h analytical hydrolysis condition is a laboratory comparison only, not this surface node's validated natural condition. No sufficiently selective natural window is claimed without conversion and survival data.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed water addition to the amide carbonyl produces a tetrahedral intermediate, followed by carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage. Net neutral-species equation: HOCH2CH(NH2)CONH2 + H2O -> HOCH2CH(NH2)COOH + NH3. Ammonia is present mainly as ammonium during acid hydrolysis; serine becomes predominantly zwitterionic after neutralization.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is established chemistry, but standard amino-acid analysis also documents that serine recovery is sensitive to hydrolysis severity (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998). That analytical literature does not validate serinamide hydrolysis in an early-Earth brine. This proposed natural completion step therefore remains speculative. Under a single-rate-limiting-step, first-order approximation, obtaining at least 95% hydrolysis while limiting independently measured serine loss to 10% requires a hydrolysis-to-loss rate ratio of about 28.4 or greater; the full two-step network requires direct kinetic fitting. If no suitable window is found, all ten pathways remain precursor networks rather than successful serine syntheses.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-fed copper/sulfide route with imine-first Strecker assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN supplies both the initial carbonyl carbon and chain-extension carbons. Copper/sulfide photochemistry supplies glycolaldehyde, followed by imine capture and the shared, conditional acid-hydrolysis endpoint.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-fed copper/sulfide route with cyanohydrin-first Strecker assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"The same sulfide-supported carbon supply first accumulates glyceronitrile. Later ammonia delivery redirects this reservoir into serine aminonitrile through reversible carbonyl/imine chemistry. Hydrolysis remains the shared completion bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-fed copper/sulfide route with imine-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied formaldehyde bypasses initial HCN reduction. Cyanohydrin formation and copper/sulfide photoreduction generate glycolaldehyde for imine-first Strecker chemistry. External formaldehyde supply is an explicit assumption, not an additional reaction.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-fed copper/sulfide route with cyanohydrin-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Imported formaldehyde is homologated using only the copper/sulfide module. Cyanide-first delivery gives glyceronitrile before ammonia-dependent reservoir exchange. This is an order-of-addition alternative, not an independent aminonitrile-forming mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-fed sulfite/ferrocyanide route with imine-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Both photoreductions use the sulfite/ferrocyanide module. Formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde adduct-release gates are explicit. No copper/sulfide stream is introduced. Success requires measured aldehyde-release flux and the shared serine-preserving hydrolysis window.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-fed sulfite/ferrocyanide route with cyanohydrin-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Sulfite photochemistry supplies glycolaldehyde through two explicitly represented aldehyde reservoirs. Released glycolaldehyde is captured as glyceronitrile before ammonia delivery. Sulfite-adduct exchange, cyanohydrin exchange and final hydrolysis are separate kinetic requirements.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-fed sulfite/ferrocyanide route with imine-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"External formaldehyde is captured as glycolonitrile before introduction into the sulfite photochemical module. Glycolaldehyde-adduct release supplies imine-first Strecker chemistry. This bypasses the first photoreduction but does not resolve downstream substrate-availability or hydrolysis limitations.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-fed sulfite/ferrocyanide route with cyanohydrin-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde-derived glycolonitrile undergoes sulfite-mediated photoreduction. After measured adduct release, cyanide-first capture and later ammonia delivery produce serine aminonitrile. No compatibility with copper/sulfide carryover is assumed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde-fed imine-first Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied glycolaldehyde enters the shortest imine-first route. This is a valid conditional feedstock route, not a claim that natural glycolaldehyde delivery has been established. It removes photochemical dependencies but retains aminonitrile formation and both hydrolysis bottlenecks.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde-fed cyanohydrin-first Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied glycolaldehyde encounters cyanide before ammonia, populating glyceronitrile. Subsequent reservoir exchange gives the same serine aminonitrile. This route tests reagent-order effects without requiring a photochemical module, but it is not mechanistically independent of P9.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_016\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_015\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Serine (DL-serine; no enantioenrichment assumed)", "overall_strategy": "Construct glycolaldehyde by experimentally motivated cyanide photoreduction and homologation, then install the amino and carboxyl groups through Strecker chemistry and explicitly resolved nitrile-to-amide-to-acid hydrolysis. Ten distinct graph routes combine five feedstock/photochemical entries with two Strecker order-of-addition branches. These are overlapping alternatives, not ten independently demonstrated syntheses; the cyanohydrin-fed branch ultimately accesses the same imine chemistry. Formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde may enter as externally supplied simple feedstocks despite also being internally formed hub intermediates. Sulfide/copper and sulfite/ferrocyanide modules are kept separate: no pathway switches between them. Sulfite-bound aldehydes are represented explicitly rather than assumed fully available. Hydantoin chemistry is excluded because it adds unresolved ring-opening and decarbamoylation bottlenecks. All modeled transformations are anoxic and use surface settings; hydrothermal labels are not used to imply successful hydrolysis. The graph contains 17 molecule nodes and 13 reaction nodes to retain chemically important adducts and the amide intermediate. It is complete for the intended transformations, not an exhaustive side-reaction network or a validated natural synthesis. In particular, a sufficiently acidic, serine-preserving natural hydrolysis window remains unverified. Numerical operating ranges below are proposed screening conditions unless identified as analytical benchmarks, not reported reaction-specific yields or rate constants. Neutral organic formulas represent equilibrating protonation pools; acid-base counterions are omitted, while the explicitly ionic sulfur equations conserve charge.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "Octasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur; terminal coproduct", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Ammonia", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxyacetonitrile", "common_name": "Glycolonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "HSO3^-", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen sulfite", "common_name": "Bisulfite; protonation-state representative of dissolved sulfur(IV)", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "S2O6^2-", "iupac_name": "Dithionate", "common_name": "Dithionate; modeled sulfur oxidation coproduct", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "CH3O4S^-", "iupac_name": "Hydroxymethanesulfonate", "common_name": "Formaldehyde-bisulfite adduct", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C2H5O5S^-", "iupac_name": "1,2-Dihydroxyethane-1-sulfonate", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde-bisulfite adduct", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C2H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-Iminoethan-1-ol", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde imine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C3H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2,3-Dihydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "Glyceronitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H5NO2/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h3,5-6H,2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C3H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "Serine aminonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2O/c4-1-3(5)2-6/h3,6H,2,5H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C3H8N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-hydroxypropanamide", "common_name": "Serinamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H8N2O2/c4-2(1-6)3(5)7/h2,6H,1,4H2,(H2,5,7)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C3H7NO3", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-Serine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-5)3(6)7/h2,5H,1,4H2,(H,6,7)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Sulfide-supported photoreduction of hydrogen cyanide to formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Photoactive copper(I)-cyanide complexes; dissolved speciation must be verified"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Copper(I)-cyanide complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 6.8-7.5; total cyanide 10-50 mM; total dissolved sulfide 10-50 mM; total Cu(I) 0.05-0.2 mM; cyanide-to-copper ratio at least 50. Use a 0.5-2 mm illuminated water layer and 240-280 nm radiation, with 254 nm as a laboratory proxy. Screen incident photon fluxes of 10^14-10^15 photons cm^-2 s^-1 and sample over 0.5-12 h rather than prescribing complete conversion. Exclude O2. Measure dissolved copper complexes, CuCN/Cu2S precipitation, sulfur turbidity, cyanide loss and aldehyde formation."}, "mechanism": "Photochemical electron transfer reduces the nitrile carbon, followed by hydrolysis of the reduced nitrogen-containing intermediate. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. Formal net equation: 8 HCN + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 CH2O + 8 NH3 + S8. S8 represents the eventual zero-valent sulfur pool, not necessarily the initially formed sulfur species.", "reasoning": "Copper/cyanide photoredox chemistry and sulfide-supported precursor synthesis are motivated by Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). The stated numerical screen is not asserted to reproduce a published yield. Total added copper does not establish active catalyst concentration: cyanide complexation competes with copper-sulfide precipitation. Polysulfides, thiocyanate formation, HCN volatilization and optical attenuation can reduce productive flux. A usable stream requires measured carbon and sulfur balances; generic addition of a sulfide mineral would not establish this chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Capture of formaldehyde as glycolonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 7.0-8.5; formaldehyde equivalents 1-20 mM; available total cyanide 5-50 mM; residence 0.5-24 h. Conduct capture in a shaded compartment to separate cyanohydrin equilibration from photochemistry. Formaldehyde hydration and, in the sulfite branch, hydroxymethanesulfonate dissociation must be included in substrate accounting. No selective removal of dissolved metal complexes by settling is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the accessible formaldehyde carbonyl and proton transfer gives HOCH2CN. Net equation: CH2O + HCN -> HOCH2CN. The reaction draws from equilibrating hydrated formaldehyde rather than requiring all formaldehyde to be unhydrated.", "reasoning": "Glycolonitrile formation is the established carbon-chain-extension entry used in cyanide photoredox networks (Ritson and Sutherland, 2012; Patel et al., 2015). Its plausibility does not imply quantitative capture in dilute natural waters. For sulfite-derived formaldehyde, rxn_005 must supply sufficient dissociation flux; the adduct cannot simply be counted as free aldehyde."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Sulfide-supported photoreduction of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Photoactive copper(I)-cyanide complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Copper(I)-cyanide complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: glycolonitrile 1-20 mM; total sulfide 10-50 mM; Cu(I) 0.05-0.2 mM; pH 6.8-7.5. Maintain and measure the cyanide ligand pool rather than assuming the catalyst survives cyanide depletion. Use 240-280 nm light, a 0.5-2 mm optical path and incident flux 10^14-10^15 photons cm^-2 s^-1. Sample over 0.5-12 h and withdraw at the measured glycolaldehyde maximum. Transfer to a shaded, cooler Strecker compartment; do not introduce ferrocyanide/sulfite chemistry."}, "mechanism": "Two-electron reduction of the nitrile followed by imine hydrolysis converts the nitrile carbon to an aldehyde while retaining the hydroxymethyl group. Formal net equation: 8 HOCH2CN + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 HOCH2CHO + 8 NH3 + S8.", "reasoning": "This homologation is central to experimentally motivated cyanosulfidic precursor chemistry (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). Glycolaldehyde accumulation competes with further cyanohydrin formation, reduction, sulfur chemistry and photochemical loss. A measured irradiation endpoint is therefore necessary. Transport must preserve the product and characterize residual copper and sulfide; shading stops irradiation but does not remove dissolved reagents."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreduction of HCN with formaldehyde-bisulfite capture", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ferrocyanide, [Fe(CN)6]^4-, as a photoredox mediator"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Ferrocyanide photoredox system", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: HCN equivalents 5-20 mM; total dissolved sulfur(IV) 50-150 mM; ferrocyanide 0.1-1 mM; pH 7.0-8.0. Determine the HSO3-/SO3^2- distribution at the actual ionic strength. Use an anoxic 0.5-2 mm illuminated layer, 240-280 nm light and incident flux 10^14-10^15 photons cm^-2 s^-1; sample over 0.5-12 h. This is a separate copper-free catchment or experimental stream, not a downstream switch from rxn_001."}, "mechanism": "Photogenerated reducing equivalents reduce HCN, and the resulting formaldehyde is reversibly trapped by hydrogen sulfite. Sulfur-centered radical coupling provides a possible anoxic donor-oxidation closure. Modeled net equation: HCN + 3 HSO3^- + H2O -> HOCH2SO3^- + NH3 + S2O6^2-. Two sulfur(IV) equivalents supply electrons; the third forms the carbonyl adduct.", "reasoning": "Sulfite/ferrocyanide-mediated HCN homologation is supported as a reaction family by Xu et al. (2018). Explicit adduct capture prevents overestimating free formaldehyde. Dithionate is a chemically plausible anoxic sulfite-radical termination product, but its exclusive formation is not established here; this sulfur closure is a model assumption requiring product analysis. Sulfate or other measured sulfur products would require corresponding additional balanced branches. The abundance and photochemical persistence of natural ferrocyanide also remain environmental constraints."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Release of formaldehyde from hydroxymethanesulfonate", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed release screen: pH 8.0-9.0, with dilution or continued cyanohydrin trapping used to shift the equilibrium. Measure total sulfur(IV), its protonation distribution, free-plus-hydrated formaldehyde, adduct concentration and dissociation kinetics over 0.5-48 h. Define K_app = [adduct]/([unbound formaldehyde pool][HSO3-]) at the actual temperature and ionic strength. The unbound fraction is 1/(1 + K_app[HSO3-]) under this two-pool approximation. Evaporation is not treated as sulfite removal."}, "mechanism": "Reverse bisulfite addition releases an equilibrating formaldehyde/hydrate pool. Net equation: HOCH2SO3^- -> CH2O + HSO3^-. This node shows the productive direction of a reversible equilibrium, coupled to formaldehyde consumption in rxn_002.", "reasoning": "Reversible carbonyl trapping is relevant to sulfite-based homologation chemistry (Xu et al., 2018), but that reaction-family citation does not supply a release constant for this proposed matrix. Productive connection requires the measured adduct-release flux to sustain glycolonitrile formation within the residence time. Dilution lowers both inhibitor and substrate concentrations and is not a free concentration mechanism. The proposed environmental release operation is conditional, not demonstrated."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreduction of glycolonitrile with glycolaldehyde-bisulfite capture", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ferrocyanide, [Fe(CN)6]^4-, as a photoredox mediator"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Ferrocyanide photoredox system", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: glycolonitrile 1-20 mM; total sulfur(IV) 50-150 mM; ferrocyanide 0.1-1 mM; pH 7.0-8.0. Use the irradiation geometry and flux range of rxn_004, with a separately measured absorption profile after the first stage. Sample over 0.5-12 h. Account for accumulated dithionate, ferro/ferricyanide speciation and aldehyde adducts. No copper/sulfide stream is introduced."}, "mechanism": "Photoreduction and hydrolysis of the nitrile generate glycolaldehyde, followed by reversible bisulfite capture. Modeled net equation: HOCH2CN + 3 HSO3^- + H2O -> HOCH2CH(OH)SO3^- + NH3 + S2O6^2-.", "reasoning": "Sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemical homologation supplies a supported alternative to the copper/sulfide module (Xu et al., 2018). The adduct is a reservoir, not automatically either a dead-end sink or freely available substrate. As in rxn_004, the dithionate-only electron-donor closure is provisional and must be checked experimentally. Productive selectivity, catalyst persistence and natural actinic flux cannot be inferred solely from the balanced equation."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Release of glycolaldehyde from its bisulfite adduct", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed release screen: pH 8.3-9.2, shaded conditions, 0.5-48 h observation. Quantify adduct, unbound glycolaldehyde including its hydrate pool, and dissolved sulfur(IV). Measure K_app and the adduct dissociation rate in the actual salt matrix. Under a two-pool equilibrium, K_app[HSO3-] <= 1 gives at least 50% sulfite-unbound glycolaldehyde; if this criterion is not met, independently demonstrate adequate release flux into the consuming Strecker reaction. Hydration and oligomerization require additional corrections."}, "mechanism": "Reversible carbon-sulfur bond cleavage regenerates glycolaldehyde and hydrogen sulfite. Net equation: HOCH2CH(OH)SO3^- -> HOCH2CHO + HSO3^-. Subsequent imine or cyanohydrin formation can pull the equilibrium without requiring instantaneous complete dissociation.", "reasoning": "This explicit gate addresses the substrate-availability limitation of sulfite photochemistry (Xu et al., 2018). No numerical equilibrium constant or rate is invented. The natural connection remains speculative until those measurements establish adequate flux. Raising pH may facilitate release but also accelerates competing glycolaldehyde chemistry; dilution can improve the unbound fraction while making downstream encounters slower. Sulfite and ferrocyanide carryover must be tested rather than assumed compatible."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Reversible formation of glycolaldehyde imine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.3-9.2; unbound glycolaldehyde equivalents 1-20 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 50-200 mM. Calculate free NH3 using temperature- and ionic-strength-corrected acid-base equilibria. Use shaded water and couple promptly to rxn_009; do not require isolation of an imine from bulk water. Screen 0.5-24 h while measuring carbonyl depletion, aminonitrile accumulation and oligomeric losses. Analyze residual copper, sulfur(IV), sulfide and iron-cyanide species in the appropriate upstream matrix."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia addition forms a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to HOCH2CH=NH. Net equation: HOCH2CHO + NH3 -> HOCH2CH=NH + H2O. The imine may remain at low steady-state abundance and be consumed through its protonated iminium form.", "reasoning": "Imine formation followed by cyanide addition is the classical Strecker mechanism (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957), applied to the glycolaldehyde precursor pool considered in cyanosulfidic chemistry (Patel et al., 2015). The proposed concentrations are screening values, not a claimed natural inventory. Hydration, ammonia protonation and competing carbonyl reactions prevent assuming quantitative imine formation."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Cyanide addition to glycolaldehyde imine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.3-9.2; available total cyanide 5-50 mM; ammonia-rich conditions inherited from rxn_008. Correct cyanide availability for metal complexation, volatilization and sulfur reactions. Use shaded conditions and follow aminonitrile formation over 0.5-24 h. Transfer the aminonitrile-containing stream promptly to a separately evaluated hydrolysis setting; do not assume storage stability or quantitative recovery during transport."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon, and proton transfer gives HOCH2CH(NH2)CN. Net equation: HOCH2CH=NH + HCN -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CN. Attack on either face produces a racemic aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the chemically direct Strecker route to the correctly connected serine aminonitrile (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957; Patel et al., 2015). It installs the future carboxyl carbon without requiring selective oxidation of a polyfunctional precursor. Retro-Strecker chemistry, oligomerization and cyanide sequestration still require measurement. Formation of this precursor alone is not evidence that the serine endpoint has been achieved."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Cyanohydrin-first capture of glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 6.8-8.0; unbound glycolaldehyde equivalents 1-20 mM; total available cyanide 5-50 mM; initially low free ammonia. Screen 0.5-24 h in shaded water. In sulfite-derived mixtures, quantify simultaneous bisulfite-adduct and cyanohydrin populations instead of treating all total glycolaldehyde as reactive carbonyl."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition to glycolaldehyde produces HOCH2CH(OH)CN. Net equation: HOCH2CHO + HCN -> HOCH2CH(OH)CN. Addition to either carbonyl face gives racemic glyceronitrile.", "reasoning": "Aldehyde cyanohydrin formation is established chemistry and is integral to the homologation systems discussed by Ritson and Sutherland (2012) and Patel et al. (2015). This branch represents a chemically meaningful cyanide-first reservoir when ammonia arrives later. It is not asserted to outperform imine-first Strecker chemistry, and direct hydrolysis of this hydroxynitrile would give glyceric acid rather than serine."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Ammonia-driven conversion of glyceronitrile to serine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: glyceronitrile 1-20 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 50-200 mM; pH 8.3-9.2; shaded, anoxic conditions. Follow cyanohydrin, aldehyde and aminonitrile concentrations over 1-48 h. Retain a measurable cyanide pool, including HCN released by cyanohydrin dissociation. Reject the route if hydrolysis, polymerization or carbonyl loss dominates before aminonitrile accumulation."}, "mechanism": "This is a net reservoir-exchange reaction, not direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group by ammonia. Glyceronitrile dissociates reversibly to glycolaldehyde and HCN; ammonia forms the imine, which is captured by cyanide through the chemistry of rxn_008 and rxn_009. Net equation: HOCH2CH(OH)CN + NH3 -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CN + H2O.", "reasoning": "The net conversion follows reversible cyanohydrin and Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850; Miller, 1957). Its operation with this substrate under the stated dilute, saline conditions is a conditional extrapolation, not a reaction-specific demonstrated natural synthesis. This branch is included because cyanide-first delivery can populate a real reservoir, but it is not mechanistically independent of the imine-first branch. Reservoir exchange and serine-aminonitrile yield must be measured."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of serine aminonitrile to serinamide", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanogenic chloride brine"], "conditions": {"temperature": "363-370 K; proposed natural-window screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Br\u00f8nsted acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed, unvalidated screen: 1-3 M analytical HCl in a concentrated volcanogenic brine; measure proton activity rather than equating it to concentration. Sample at 1, 3, 6, 12, 24 and 48 h. Determine aminonitrile disappearance, serinamide formation, serine formation and identified degradation products independently. Use anoxic, shaded conditions. Measure upstream carryover and run matrix-matched controls; neither settling nor dilution is assumed to selectively remove dissolved copper or iron-cyanide complexes. The occurrence and persistence of such concentrated natural acid are unresolved."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation activates the nitrile carbon toward water addition. Proton transfers and tautomerization give the primary amide. Net equation: HOCH2CH(NH2)CN + H2O -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CONH2. The amino group is predominantly protonated in the reaction medium.", "reasoning": "Acidic conversion of aminonitriles to amino acids is established at the reaction-family level (Miller, 1957), but this does not establish a serine-preserving environmental window for this substrate. This particular operating window is explicitly speculative and is a key completion bottleneck. Resolving the amide prevents a fast hydration step from being mistaken for complete carboxyl formation. A coupled kinetic model must fit aminonitrile-to-amide, amide-to-serine and loss reactions; precursor disappearance alone is insufficient."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Hydrolysis of serinamide to serine with explicit survival assessment", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanogenic chloride brine"], "conditions": {"temperature": "363-370 K; proposed natural-window screen", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Br\u00f8nsted acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: 1-6 M analytical HCl, with separate 6, 12, 24, 48 and 96 h samples rather than an assumed residence time. Quantify serine directly against an authentic standard and expose authentic serine to the same brine to measure loss. Cool withdrawn product streams to 300-310 K. The familiar 6 M HCl, 383 K, approximately 24 h analytical hydrolysis condition is a laboratory comparison only, not this surface node's validated natural condition. No sufficiently selective natural window is claimed without conversion and survival data."}, "mechanism": "Acid-catalyzed water addition to the amide carbonyl produces a tetrahedral intermediate, followed by carbon-nitrogen bond cleavage. Net neutral-species equation: HOCH2CH(NH2)CONH2 + H2O -> HOCH2CH(NH2)COOH + NH3. Ammonia is present mainly as ammonium during acid hydrolysis; serine becomes predominantly zwitterionic after neutralization.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is established chemistry, but standard amino-acid analysis also documents that serine recovery is sensitive to hydrolysis severity (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998). That analytical literature does not validate serinamide hydrolysis in an early-Earth brine. This proposed natural completion step therefore remains speculative. Under a single-rate-limiting-step, first-order approximation, obtaining at least 95% hydrolysis while limiting independently measured serine loss to 10% requires a hydrolysis-to-loss rate ratio of about 28.4 or greater; the full two-step network requires direct kinetic fitting. If no suitable window is found, all ten pathways remain precursor networks rather than successful serine syntheses."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "HCN-fed copper/sulfide route with imine-f