m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Serine (DL-serine; no enantioenrichment assumed)\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct glycolaldehyde by experimentally motivated cyanide photoreduction and homologation, then install the amino and carboxyl groups through Strecker chemistry and explicitly resolved nitrile-to-amide-to-acid hydrolysis. Ten distinct graph routes combine five feedstock/photochemical entries with two Strecker order-of-addition branches. These are overlapping alternatives, not ten independently demonstrated syntheses; the cyanohydrin-fed branch ultimately accesses the same imine chemistry. Formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde may enter as externally supplied simple feedstocks despite also being internally formed hub intermediates. Sulfide/copper and sulfite/ferrocyanide modules are kept separate: no pathway switches between them. Sulfite-bound aldehydes are represented explicitly rather than assumed fully available. Hydantoin chemistry is excluded because it adds unresolved ring-opening and decarbamoylation bottlenecks. All modeled transformations are anoxic and use surface settings; hydrothermal labels are not used to imply successful hydrolysis. The graph contains 17 molecule nodes and 13 reaction nodes to retain chemically important adducts and the amide intermediate. It is complete for the intended transformations, not an exhaustive side-reaction network or a validated natural synthesis. In particular, a sufficiently acidic, serine-preserving natural hydrolysis window remains unverified. Numerical operating ranges below are proposed screening conditions unless identified as analytical benchmarks, not reported reaction-specific yields or rate constants. Neutral organic formulas represent equilibrating protonation pools; acid-base counterions are omitted, while the explicitly ionic sulfur equations conserve charge.\n\nMOLECULES (17 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_004: S8 (Elemental sulfur; terminal coproduct) [intermediate]\n  - mol_005: H3N (Ammonia) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: CH2O (Formaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C2H3NO (Glycolonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C2H4O2 (Glycolaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: HSO3^- (Bisulfite; protonation-state representative of dissolved sulfur(IV)) [starting]\n  - mol_010: S2O6^2- (Dithionate; modeled sulfur oxidation coproduct) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: CH3O4S^- (Formaldehyde-bisulfite adduct) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C2H5O5S^- (Glycolaldehyde-bisulfite adduct) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C2H5NO (Glycolaldehyde imine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C3H5NO2 (Glyceronitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C3H6N2O (Serine aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C3H8N2O2 (Serinamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C3H7NO3 (DL-Serine) [target]\n\nREACTIONS (13 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_006, mol_005, mol_004 (Sulfide-supported photoreduction of hydrogen cyanide to formaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_006, mol_001 -> mol_007 (Capture of formaldehyde as glycolonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_007, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_008, mol_005, mol_004 (Sulfide-supported photoreduction of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_001, mol_009, mol_003 -> mol_011, mol_005, mol_010 (Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreduction of HCN with formaldehyde-bisulfite capture) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_011 -> mol_006, mol_009 (Release of formaldehyde from hydroxymethanesulfonate) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_007, mol_009, mol_003 -> mol_012, mol_005, mol_010 (Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreduction of glycolonitrile with glycolaldehyde-bisulfite capture) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_012 -> mol_008, mol_009 (Release of glycolaldehyde from its bisulfite adduct) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_008, mol_005 -> mol_013, mol_003 (Reversible formation of glycolaldehyde imine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_013, mol_001 -> mol_015 (Cyanide addition to glycolaldehyde imine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_008, mol_001 -> mol_014 (Cyanohydrin-first capture of glycolaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_014, mol_005 -> mol_015, mol_003 (Ammonia-driven conversion of glyceronitrile to serine aminonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_015, mol_003 -> mol_016 (Acid-catalyzed hydration of serine aminonitrile to serinamide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_013: mol_016, mol_003 -> mol_017, mol_005 (Hydrolysis of serinamide to serine with explicit survival assessment) [Surface]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: HCN-fed copper/sulfide route with imine-first Strecker assembly (7 steps)\n  - P2: HCN-fed copper/sulfide route with cyanohydrin-first Strecker assembly (7 steps)\n  - P3: Formaldehyde-fed copper/sulfide route with imine-first assembly (6 steps)\n  - P4: Formaldehyde-fed copper/sulfide route with cyanohydrin-first assembly (6 steps)\n  - P5: HCN-fed sulfite/ferrocyanide route with imine-first assembly (9 steps)\n  - P6: HCN-fed sulfite/ferrocyanide route with cyanohydrin-first assembly (9 steps)\n  - P7: Formaldehyde-fed sulfite/ferrocyanide route with imine-first assembly (7 steps)\n  - P8: Formaldehyde-fed sulfite/ferrocyanide route with cyanohydrin-first assembly (7 steps)\n  - P9: Glycolaldehyde-fed imine-first Strecker 