onia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"O3S-2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfite\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Sulfite ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"HO3S-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogensulfite\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Bisulfite ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"O4S-2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Sulfate ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3O4S-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxymethanesulfonate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Formaldehyde bisulfite adduct\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5O5S-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1,2-dihydroxyethane-1-sulfonate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycolaldehyde bisulfite adduct\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-iminoethan-1-ol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycolaldehyde imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glyceronitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H8N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Serinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-Serine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-6)3(5)7/h2,6H,1,4H2,(H,5,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen(1+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"O2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfur dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Sulfur dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/O2S/c1-3-2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-driven photoreduction of hydrogen cyanide to formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved Cu(I)-cyanide complexes generated from a CuCN source under cyanide excess\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 7.5-8.5; total cyanide 10-50 mM; total dissolved sulfide 5-50 mM; dissolved Cu 10-100 micromolar; measured free cyanide/Cu molar ratio at least 20. Irradiate 0.5-2 mm water layers for 4-48 h using a 254 nm laboratory benchmark of 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1. Translate to natural 200-280 nm sunlight only with a measured action spectrum and site-specific photon dose. Measure HCN/H2S volatilization and CuS precipitation; total copper is not a proxy for active catalyst.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Productive-channel equation: 8 HCN + 8 H2O + 8 H2S -> 8 CH2O + 8 NH3 + S8. Photoinduced electron transfer reduces the nitrile through imine-level intermediates; hydrolysis releases formaldehyde. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. S8 represents the elemental-sulfur endpoint rather than an assertion that all sulfur immediately forms S8.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyanosulfidic photochemistry provides experimental precedent for reductive access to aldehydic and amino-acid precursors (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). The numerical ranges are a proposed screening envelope, not reported serine yields. Copper sulfide precipitation, sulfur/polysulfide formation, thiocyanate production, and aldehyde overprocessing compete with the productive channel. An open pool is viable only if measured supply offsets volatile losses. No iron sulfide mineral is assigned an unsupported catalytic role.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde capture as glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; total formaldehyde 1-20 mM; total cyanide 5-50 mM; 0.5-24 h in a shaded aqueous compartment. Quantify free carbonyl, hydrate, cyanohydrin, and volatile carbon losses. Supplied formaldehyde is an allowed entry feedstock. Do not assume evaporation concentrates unbound HCN.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"CH2O + HCN -> HOCH2CN. Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer. Formaldehyde hydration and cyanohydrin formation are reversible.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolonitrile formation and its use in reductive homologation have direct experimental precedent (Ritson and Sutherland, 2012; Patel et al., 2015). The relevant kinetic comparison is carbonyl capture versus volatilization and competing reactions. This edge can receive either externally supplied formaldehyde or formaldehyde released by rxn_001 or rxn_005.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide-driven photoreductive homologation of glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: glycolonitrile 1-10 mM; total sulfide 5-50 mM; dissolved Cu 10-100 micromolar; pH 7.5-8.5; free cyanide/Cu ratio at least 20. Use 254 nm at 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1 for a 4-48 h dose-response series. Stop exposure at the measured glycolaldehyde maximum, then move by flow into a shaded downstream pool. Test actual metal/sulfide carryover in the subsequent Strecker mixture rather than assuming purification.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"8 HOCH2CN + 8 H2O + 8 H2S -> 8 HOCH2CHO + 8 NH3 + S8. Nitrile photoreduction followed by imine hydrolysis produces the C2 aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a supported cyanosulfidic homologation reaction family (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). Productive selectivity depends on irradiation dose, dissolved catalyst speciation, and sulfide availability. Natural advection into shaded pores is a possible staging mechanism, but its residence-time distribution and carbon recovery must be measured. This pathway does not subsequently switch to ferrocyanide/sulfite photochemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreduction of HCN with formaldehyde trapping\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved hexacyanidoferrate(II), ferrocyanide\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ferrocyanide-mediated photoredox system with sulfur(IV) reductant\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: total cyanide 5-20 mM; total sulfur(IV) 20-100 mM; ferrocyanide 10-100 micromolar; pH 6.8-8.0. Sulfite/bisulfite fractions follow the measured pH and ionic strength. Irradiate at 254 nm, 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1, for 4-72 h with sulfur and carbon time courses. Use an independent stream without added copper or sulfide. In an Fe2+-rich catchment, measure ferrocyanide precipitation and dissolved photoredox activity rather than assuming soluble catalyst availability.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN + 2 H2O + SO3^2- + HSO3^- -> HOCH2SO3^- + NH3 + SO4^2-. Nitrile reduction/hydrolysis supplies formaldehyde, which is captured by bisulfite. The equation describes the sulfate-forming productive channel; sulfate oxygen can originate from water, so O2 is not required.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Sulfite/ferrocyanide-mediated reductive homologation and bisulfite-bound carbonyl reservoirs have experimental support (Xu et al., 2018). The assigned concentrations and exposure times are proposed tests. Sulfate is not assumed to be the exclusive oxidized sulfur product: dithionate and other sulfur products must be included in experimental mass balances. Ferrocyanide availability and photoligand loss remain environmental constraints, especially where dissolved Fe2+ is abundant.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-assisted formaldehyde release with sulfur dioxide escape\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed, unvalidated recovery stage: pH 1.0-2.0 established by anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing condensate after accounting for sulfur(IV) alkalinity; 0.5-48 h with natural gas bubbling or surface gas exchange. Quantify SO2 escape, formaldehyde co-loss, and residual sulfur(IV). After transfer to ammonia-buffered water, require measured K_add[HSO3^-] <= 1 for at least 50% unbound carbonyl under a simple 1:1 binding model. A preliminary residual sulfur(IV) target is 1-10 micromolar, but the measured equilibrium constant controls the actual requirement.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HOCH2SO3^- + H+ -> CH2O + SO2 + H2O. This is a net dissociation, protonation, and gas-transfer operation, not a single elementary reaction. Lower sulfur(IV) activity can shift the carbonyl-adduct equilibrium toward formaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Xu et al. (2018) support the existence and relevance of bisulfite reservoirs, not this proposed natural recovery protocol. Acid-assisted degassing is therefore a speculative process edge. Its purpose is to avoid assuming unrestricted formaldehyde availability in sulfite-rich water. A useful release window requires both a measured dissociation flux and acceptable carbon recovery; for an approximately first-order release, k_release times residence time >= 2.3 is a screening criterion for 90% release, not an asserted result. Persistent adduct retention or formaldehyde stripping can invalidate P5 and P6.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite/ferrocyanide homologation of glycolonitrile with glycolaldehyde trapping\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved hexacyanidoferrate(II), ferrocyanide\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ferrocyanide-mediated photoredox system with sulfur(IV) reductant\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: glycolonitrile 1-10 mM; total sulfur(IV) 20-100 mM; ferrocyanide 10-100 micromolar; pH 6.8-8.0; 254 nm irradiation at 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1 for 4-72 h. Determine the glycolaldehyde-adduct maximum, catalyst speciation, sulfate/dithionate distribution, and organic side-product balance. For material arriving from rxn_005, first verify carbon recovery and pH adjustment; renewed sulfur(IV) supply is explicit in the inputs.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HOCH2CN + 2 H2O + SO3^2- + HSO3^- -> HOCH2CH(OH)SO3^- + NH3 + SO4^2-. Reduction and hydrolysis create glycolaldehyde, followed by reversible bisulfite addition.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Sulfite/ferrocyanide reductive homologation is experimentally supported (Xu et al., 2018). Representing the adduct prevents equating total glycolaldehyde-derived carbon with free glycolaldehyde. The net equation is balanced for the sulfate channel but is not a complete prediction of sulfur photoproducts. This branch uses a sulfur(IV) stream rather than relying on untested mixing with copper/sulfide photochemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-assisted glycolaldehyde release with sulfur dioxide escape\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313-333 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: pH 1.0-2.0 from anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing condensate; 0.5-48 h of gas exchange. Measure free glycolaldehyde, bound glycolaldehyde, SO2 loss, and organic degradation. Before Strecker assembly, transfer to an ammonia-rich pool and require K_add[HSO3^-] <= 1 at the new pH, together with a measured release flux sufficient to supply the intended 1-20 mM carbonyl pool. Residual total sulfur(IV) of 1-10 micromolar is a screening target, not a demonstrated outcome.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HOCH2CH(OH)SO3^- + H+ -> HOCH2CHO + SO2 + H2O. Reversible adduct cleavage is coupled to protonation and physical SO2 removal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Bisulfite reservoir chemistry is supported by Xu et al. (2018); efficient natural acid-degassing recovery of this specific reservoir is not established by that citation and is speculative here. Glycolaldehyde availability must be determined from equilibrium and kinetic measurements, not total carbonyl-derived carbon. Acid supply, mineral neutralization, aldehyde survival, and gas-transfer rates are decisive constraints. If acceptable release cannot be demonstrated, the sulfite-fed pathways do not reach the common Strecker stage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Transient glycolaldehyde imine formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed screen: glycolaldehyde-derived carbon 1-20 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 0.1-1.0 M; pH 8.5-9.2; 1-72 h in a shaded wet pore or pool. The imine is transient and is not isolated or assumed to dominate the solution. Couple this reaction immediately to rxn_009. Measure competing cyanohydrin, oligomer, and carbonyl-adduct pools in the actual upstream matrix.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HOCH2CHO + NH3 -> HOCH2CH=NH + H2O. Ammonia addition gives a carbinolamine, followed by dehydration. The aqueous equilibrium is pulled toward productive flux by cyanide trapping in rxn_009.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imine formation followed by cyanide capture is the mechanistic basis of Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). Prebiotic access to relevant aldehydic and amino-acid precursors is supported by Patel et al. (2015). Neither citation establishes the proposed imine concentration or rate in a sulfur-containing natural mixture. High ammonia activity is a conditional concentrated-pool requirement, and competing glycolaldehyde reactions must be quantified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of the imine to form serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Operate concurrently with rxn_008: pH 8.5-9.2; total cyanide 5-50 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 0.1-1.0 M; sample over 1-72 h. Verify aminonitrile identity against an authentic standard and quantify both enantiomers together. Compare the real transferred mixture with metal-free and sulfur(IV)-free controls. A proposed screening gate is less than 20% reduction in aminonitrile recovery attributable to upstream carryover; this is an acceptance criterion, not an observed result.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HOCH2CH=NH + HCN -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CN. Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon, establishing the serine carbon skeleton and producing a racemic center without a chiral influence.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The transformation is recognizable Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), consistent with cyanosulfidic access to amino-acid precursors (Patel et al., 2015). It identifies the correct serine aminonitrile rather than an isomeric amino alcohol or beta-amino nitrile. Productive formation does not establish successful amino-acid release; rxn_012 and rxn_013 remain separate completion bottlenecks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde capture as glyceronitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed order-of-addition screen: glycolaldehyde 1-20 mM; total cyanide 5-50 mM; pH 7.5-8.5; 1-24 h before substantial ammonia addition. Quantify the equilibrium cyanohydrin pool and residual aldehyde. Use a shaded compartment to avoid unintended photoreduction of the new nitrile.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HOCH2CHO + HCN -> HOCH2CH(OH)CN. Reversible cyanide addition generates glyceronitrile, the glycolaldehyde cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolaldehyde cyanohydrin formation is chemically well founded within reductive-homologation chemistry (Ritson and Sutherland, 2012; Patel et al., 2015). This reaction stores the carbon skeleton but does not install the amino group. It is included only to examine a different order of reagent arrival, not as evidence for an independent serine-forming mechanism.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-dependent transfer from the cyanohydrin reservoir to serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed, unvalidated substrate-specific screen: glyceronitrile 1-20 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 0.2-1.0 M; pH 8.8-9.5; 12-168 h in darkness. Cyanide released during cyanohydrin dissociation supplies the trapping pool. Measure glyceronitrile disappearance, free aldehyde, aminonitrile appearance, and irreversible carbon loss. Require positive net aminonitrile production rather than merely rapid cyanohydrin exchange.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net equation: HOCH2CH(OH)CN + NH3 -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CN + H2O. Mechanistically, the cyanohydrin dissociates to glycolaldehyde and HCN; glycolaldehyde forms a transient imine, which is trapped by cyanide. This is not direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Strecker chemistry supports the shared imine/cyanide mechanism (Strecker, 1850), but useful net flux from this particular cyanohydrin reservoir under the proposed natural conditions is not established by the cited work. This is an explicitly speculative order-of-addition branch, not a demonstrated shortcut. It is unnecessary for P1, P3, P5, P7, and P9. Failure of this equilibration branch eliminates the other five pathway entries without invalidating imine-first assembly.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed hydration of serine aminonitrile to serinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen ions maintained by anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing condensate\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed environmental-window test: aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM; measured pH 0-1; nominal HCl 0.1-1.0 M before matrix-dependent corrections; 24-336 h in a shaded hot pool. Determine acid consumption by ammonia and minerals instead of assuming the initial pH persists. Measure aminonitrile, serinamide, serine, and degradation products at multiple times. These conditions are not claimed to be an established serine-aminonitrile operating window.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HOCH2CH(NH2)CN + H2O -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CONH2. Protonation activates the nitrile toward water addition, followed by imidic-acid/amide tautomerization. The equation uses neutral organic reference species; the amino group is predominantly protonated in the reaction pool.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acidic conversion of Strecker nitriles to amino acids has classical precedent (Strecker, 1850), but that precedent does not supply substrate-specific hydrolysis and survival rates for serine aminonitrile. This natural implementation is therefore an explicitly unvalidated extrapolation. Volcanic HCl supplies acidity without O2; sufficiently acidic, weakly buffered pools are a restrictive geochemical requirement, not a generic surface condition. A hydrothermal label or an elevated temperature alone would not resolve the kinetic bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Serinamide hydrolysis and retention of DL-serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen ions maintained by anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing condensate\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-370 K during hydrolysis; below 310 K after downstream cooling\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Test together with rxn_012 at pH 0-1 for 24-336 h; serinamide is an explicit kinetic intermediate, not an assumed isolated transfer product. Use serine-spike controls to measure destruction in the actual matrix. Fit hydrolysis rates k12 and k13 and serine-loss rate k_loss. A conservative screening condition is 2.3/k12 + 2.3/k13 < 0.223/k_loss; independently require at least 50% molar serine recovery from the initial aminonitrile. Both are proposed acceptance criteria. Natural outflow into cooler water must be included in the recovery balance.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HOCH2CH(NH2)CONH2 + H2O -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CO2H + NH3. Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis releases serine and ammonia; ammonia is predominantly ammonium under the reaction conditions. Cooling and subsequent buffering change protonation, not the serine carbon skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide hydrolysis is chemically established, but a substrate-specific natural production-versus-destruction window is not established here. Conventional amino-acid analysis often uses approximately 6 M HCl at 383 K for 24 h and documents residue-dependent losses, including serine; this is an analytical benchmark, not evidence that the proposed natural pool works (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998). The environmental implementation remains an unvalidated extrapolation. If measurable serine release requires only the harsher analytical treatment, the environmental synthesis claim must be rejected rather than substituting laboratory workup for prebiotic production.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-fed sulfide photochemistry with imine-first Strecker assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN supplies both the photoreduced carbon and homologating cyanide. Sulfide/copper photochemistry generates formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde, followed by transient-imine capture and the common conditional hydrolysis endpoint. This is a candidate integrated route, not a demonstrated natural sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-fed sulfide photochemistry with cyanohydrin-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the same sulfide photochemical entry as P1, but glycolaldehyde encounters cyanide before concentrated ammonia. Productive transfer from glyceronitrile to aminonitrile is an additional unvalidated requirement. Its elementary mechanism still includes an imine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-fed sulfide homologation with imine-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Begins with externally supplied formaldehyde, avoiding HCN-to-formaldehyde photoreduction. Glycolonitrile homologation supplies glycolaldehyde for imine-first Strecker chemistry. Feedstock availability, photochemical carryover, and the shared hydrolysis window remain conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-fed sulfide homologation with cyanohydrin-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied formaldehyde enters sulfide-driven homologation, followed by glyceronitrile capture and ammonia-dependent redistribution. This is the order-of-addition counterpart of P3 and carries the extra rxn_011 flux uncertainty.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-fed sulfite photochemistry with imine-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Two sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemical stages produce explicit formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde bisulfite reservoirs. Both proposed recovery operations must release useful carbonyl flux before imine-first Strecker assembly. No copper/sulfide stage is mixed into this pathway.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN-fed sulfite photochemistry with cyanohydrin-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the two bisulfite-reservoir recovery requirements of P5 with the additional cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile flux requirement. It is more condition-dependent than its imine-first counterpart and is not assigned an evidentiary bonus for its additional steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-fed sulfite homologation with imine-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied formaldehyde avoids the first sulfite photoreduction and formaldehyde-release operation. Glycolonitrile is homologated into the glycolaldehyde bisulfite reservoir, whose measured release must support imine-first Strecker chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde-fed sulfite homologation with cyanohydrin-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses the same supplied-formaldehyde entry as P7, followed by glycolaldehyde recovery, cyanohydrin capture, and ammonia-dependent aminonitrile formation. Both residual sulfur(IV) effects and cyanohydrin exchange must be tested quantitatively.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied glycolaldehyde with imine-first Strecker assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Begins with externally supplied glycolaldehyde, ammonia, and cyanide. This is the least assumption-intensive target-forming subnetwork under that feedstock boundary condition, because it avoids photoredox speciation, bisulfite recovery, and cyanohydrin-reservoir conversion. Its serine-producing environmental hydrolysis window nevertheless remains unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Supplied glycolaldehyde with cyanohydrin-first assembly\",\n      \"description\": \"Externally supplied glycolaldehyde first encounters cyanide, then ammonia. This tests whether a stored glyceronitrile pool can feed the same imine-dependent Strecker endpoint. It is sequence-distinct from P9 but is not an independent elementary reaction mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_015\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Serine (racemic DL-serine)", "overall_strategy": "Construct serine's carbon skeleton by glycolaldehyde Strecker chemistry, followed by explicitly resolved nitrile-to-amide and amide-to-acid hydrolysis. Five entry options are combined with two Strecker order-of-addition options to provide ten sequence-distinct pathways. These are overlapping process alternatives, not ten independently demonstrated mechanisms: both Strecker options ultimately require imine formation and cyanide capture. Formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde, ammonia, and cyanide may be externally supplied where stated. Their supply is a boundary condition, not an implied synthesis reaction. Sulfide/copper and sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry are kept in separate pathways; compatibility of mixed photochemical streams is not assumed. Numerical conditions below are proposed experimental screening ranges unless explicitly identified as established analytical practice. In particular, a naturally accessible, serine-preserving hydrolysis window remains unvalidated; no literature-supported numerical confidence of 0.90 is claimed. P9 requires the fewest unvalidated upstream operations when glycolaldehyde is supplied. All stages use anoxic surface settings, including shaded geothermal pools. Balanced equations are included in mechanism fields without repeating molecule IDs. Organic formulas use neutral reference structures; actual protonation follows the stated pH. Spectator counterions are omitted. Terminal coproducts use the schema's intermediate role because no coproduct role is available.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "octasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetonitrile", "common_name": "Glycolonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h4H,2H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "O3S-2", "iupac_name": "sulfite", "common_name": "Sulfite ion", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "HO3S-", "iupac_name": "hydrogensulfite", "common_name": "Bisulfite ion", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "O4S-2", "iupac_name": "sulfate", "common_name": "Sulfate ion", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "CH3O4S-", "iupac_name": "hydroxymethanesulfonate", "common_name": "Formaldehyde bisulfite adduct", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C2H5O5S-", "iupac_name": "1,2-dihydroxyethane-1-sulfonate", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde bisulfite adduct", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C2H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-iminoethan-1-ol", "common_name": "Glycolaldehyde imine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C3H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "Serine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C3H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "Glyceronitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C3H8N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxypropanamide", "common_name": "Serinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C3H7NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-Serine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2(1-6)3(5)7/h2,6H,1,4H2,(H,5,7)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "H+", "iupac_name": "hydrogen(1+)", "common_name": "Hydrogen ion", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "O2S", "iupac_name": "sulfur dioxide", "common_name": "Sulfur dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/O2S/c1-3-2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Sulfide-driven photoreduction of hydrogen cyanide to formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved Cu(I)-cyanide complexes generated from a CuCN source under cyanide excess"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 7.5-8.5; total cyanide 10-50 mM; total dissolved sulfide 5-50 mM; dissolved Cu 10-100 micromolar; measured free cyanide/Cu molar ratio at least 20. Irradiate 0.5-2 mm water layers for 4-48 h using a 254 nm laboratory benchmark of 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1. Translate to natural 200-280 nm sunlight only with a measured action spectrum and site-specific photon dose. Measure HCN/H2S volatilization and CuS precipitation; total copper is not a proxy for active catalyst."}, "mechanism": "Productive-channel equation: 8 HCN + 8 H2O + 8 H2S -> 8 CH2O + 8 NH3 + S8. Photoinduced electron transfer reduces the nitrile through imine-level intermediates; hydrolysis releases formaldehyde. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. S8 represents the elemental-sulfur endpoint rather than an assertion that all sulfur immediately forms S8.", "reasoning": "Cyanosulfidic photochemistry provides experimental precedent for reductive access to aldehydic and amino-acid precursors (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). The numerical ranges are a proposed screening envelope, not reported serine yields. Copper sulfide precipitation, sulfur/polysulfide formation, thiocyanate production, and aldehyde overprocessing compete with the productive channel. An open pool is viable only if measured supply offsets volatile losses. No iron sulfide mineral is assigned an unsupported catalytic role."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Formaldehyde capture as glycolonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 8.0-9.0; total formaldehyde 1-20 mM; total cyanide 5-50 mM; 0.5-24 h in a shaded aqueous compartment. Quantify free carbonyl, hydrate, cyanohydrin, and volatile carbon losses. Supplied formaldehyde is an allowed entry feedstock. Do not assume evaporation concentrates unbound HCN."}, "mechanism": "CH2O + HCN -> HOCH2CN. Cyanide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer. Formaldehyde hydration and cyanohydrin formation are reversible.", "reasoning": "Glycolonitrile formation and its use in reductive homologation have direct experimental precedent (Ritson and Sutherland, 2012; Patel et al., 2015). The relevant kinetic comparison is carbonyl capture versus volatilization and competing reactions. This edge can receive either externally supplied formaldehyde or formaldehyde released by rxn_001 or rxn_005."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Sulfide-driven photoreductive homologation of glycolonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_006", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: glycolonitrile 1-10 mM; total sulfide 5-50 mM; dissolved Cu 10-100 micromolar; pH 7.5-8.5; free cyanide/Cu ratio at least 20. Use 254 nm at 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1 for a 4-48 h dose-response series. Stop exposure at the measured glycolaldehyde maximum, then move by flow into a shaded downstream pool. Test actual metal/sulfide carryover in the subsequent Strecker mixture rather than assuming purification."}, "mechanism": "8 HOCH2CN + 8 H2O + 8 H2S -> 8 HOCH2CHO + 8 NH3 + S8. Nitrile photoreduction followed by imine hydrolysis produces the C2 aldehyde.", "reasoning": "This is a supported cyanosulfidic homologation reaction family (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). Productive selectivity depends on irradiation dose, dissolved catalyst speciation, and sulfide availability. Natural advection into shaded pores is a possible staging mechanism, but its residence-time distribution and carbon recovery must be measured. This pathway does not subsequently switch to ferrocyanide/sulfite photochemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreduction of HCN with formaldehyde trapping", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_009", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_006", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved hexacyanidoferrate(II), ferrocyanide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Ferrocyanide-mediated photoredox system with sulfur(IV) reductant", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: total cyanide 5-20 mM; total sulfur(IV) 20-100 mM; ferrocyanide 10-100 micromolar; pH 6.8-8.0. Sulfite/bisulfite fractions follow the measured pH and ionic strength. Irradiate at 254 nm, 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1, for 4-72 h with sulfur and carbon time courses. Use an independent stream without added copper or sulfide. In an Fe2+-rich catchment, measure ferrocyanide precipitation and dissolved photoredox activity rather than assuming soluble catalyst availability."}, "mechanism": "HCN + 2 H2O + SO3^2- + HSO3^- -> HOCH2SO3^- + NH3 + SO4^2-. Nitrile reduction/hydrolysis supplies formaldehyde, which is captured by bisulfite. The equation describes the sulfate-forming productive channel; sulfate oxygen can originate from water, so O2 is not required.", "reasoning": "Sulfite/ferrocyanide-mediated reductive homologation and bisulfite-bound carbonyl reservoirs have experimental support (Xu et al., 2018). The assigned concentrations and exposure times are proposed tests. Sulfate is not assumed to be the exclusive oxidized sulfur product: dithionate and other sulfur products must be included in experimental mass balances. Ferrocyanide availability and photoligand loss remain environmental constraints, especially where dissolved Fe2+ is abundant."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Acid-assisted formaldehyde release with sulfur dioxide escape", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_020", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "313-333 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed, unvalidated recovery stage: pH 1.0-2.0 established by anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing condensate after accounting for sulfur(IV) alkalinity; 0.5-48 h with natural gas bubbling or surface gas exchange. Quantify SO2 escape, formaldehyde co-loss, and residual sulfur(IV). After transfer to ammonia-buffered water, require measured K_add[HSO3^-] <= 1 for at least 50% unbound carbonyl under a simple 1:1 binding model. A preliminary residual sulfur(IV) target is 1-10 micromolar, but the measured equilibrium constant controls the actual requirement."}, "mechanism": "HOCH2SO3^- + H+ -> CH2O + SO2 + H2O. This is a net dissociation, protonation, and gas-transfer operation, not a single elementary reaction. Lower sulfur(IV) activity can shift the carbonyl-adduct equilibrium toward formaldehyde.", "reasoning": "Xu et al. (2018) support the existence and relevance of bisulfite reservoirs, not this proposed natural recovery protocol. Acid-assisted degassing is therefore a speculative process edge. Its purpose is to avoid assuming unrestricted formaldehyde availability in sulfite-rich water. A useful release window requires both a measured dissociation flux and acceptable carbon recovery; for an approximately first-order release, k_release times residence time >= 2.3 is a screening criterion for 90% release, not an asserted result. Persistent adduct retention or formaldehyde stripping can invalidate P5 and P6."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Sulfite/ferrocyanide homologation of glycolonitrile with glycolaldehyde trapping", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002", "mol_009", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_006", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved hexacyanidoferrate(II), ferrocyanide"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Ferrocyanide-mediated photoredox system with sulfur(IV) reductant", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: glycolonitrile 1-10 mM; total sulfur(IV) 20-100 mM; ferrocyanide 10-100 micromolar; pH 6.8-8.0; 254 nm irradiation at 1e14-1e15 photons cm^-2 s^-1 for 4-72 h. Determine the glycolaldehyde-adduct maximum, catalyst speciation, sulfate/dithionate distribution, and organic side-product balance. For material arriving from rxn_005, first verify carbon recovery and pH adjustment; renewed sulfur(IV) supply is explicit in the inputs."}, "mechanism": "HOCH2CN + 2 H2O + SO3^2- + HSO3^- -> HOCH2CH(OH)SO3^- + NH3 + SO4^2-. Reduction and hydrolysis create glycolaldehyde, followed by reversible bisulfite addition.", "reasoning": "Sulfite/ferrocyanide reductive homologation is experimentally supported (Xu et al., 2018). Representing the adduct prevents equating total glycolaldehyde-derived carbon with free glycolaldehyde. The net equation is balanced for the sulfate channel but is not a complete prediction of sulfur photoproducts. This branch uses a sulfur(IV) stream rather than relying on untested mixing with copper/sulfide photochemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Acid-assisted glycolaldehyde release with sulfur dioxide escape", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_019"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_020", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "313-333 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: pH 1.0-2.0 from anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing condensate; 0.5-48 h of gas exchange. Measure free glycolaldehyde, bound glycolaldehyde, SO2 loss, and organic degradation. Before Strecker assembly, transfer to an ammonia-rich pool and require K_add[HSO3^-] <= 1 at the new pH, together with a measured release flux sufficient to supply the intended 1-20 mM carbonyl pool. Residual total sulfur(IV) of 1-10 micromolar is a screening target, not a demonstrated outcome."}, "mechanism": "HOCH2CH(OH)SO3^- + H+ -> HOCH2CHO + SO2 + H2O. Reversible adduct cleavage is coupled to protonation and physical SO2 removal.", "reasoning": "Bisulfite reservoir chemistry is supported by Xu et al. (2018); efficient natural acid-degassing recovery of this specific reservoir is not established by that citation and is speculative here. Glycolaldehyde availability must be determined from equilibrium and kinetic measurements, not total carbonyl-derived carbon. Acid supply, mineral neutralization, aldehyde survival, and gas-transfer rates are decisive constraints. If acceptable release cannot be demonstrated, the sulfite-fed pathways do not reach the common Strecker stage."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Transient glycolaldehyde imine formation", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed screen: glycolaldehyde-derived carbon 1-20 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 0.1-1.0 M; pH 8.5-9.2; 1-72 h in a shaded wet pore or pool. The imine is transient and is not isolated or assumed to dominate the solution. Couple this reaction immediately to rxn_009. Measure competing cyanohydrin, oligomer, and carbonyl-adduct pools in the actual upstream matrix."}, "mechanism": "HOCH2CHO + NH3 -> HOCH2CH=NH + H2O. Ammonia addition gives a carbinolamine, followed by dehydration. The aqueous equilibrium is pulled toward productive flux by cyanide trapping in rxn_009.", "reasoning": "Imine formation followed by cyanide capture is the mechanistic basis of Strecker synthesis (Strecker, 1850). Prebiotic access to relevant aldehydic and amino-acid precursors is supported by Patel et al. (2015). Neither citation establishes the proposed imine concentration or rate in a sulfur-containing natural mixture. High ammonia activity is a conditional concentrated-pool requirement, and competing glycolaldehyde reactions must be quantified."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Cyanide capture of the imine to form serine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Operate concurrently with rxn_008: pH 8.5-9.2; total cyanide 5-50 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 0.1-1.0 M; sample over 1-72 h. Verify aminonitrile identity against an authentic standard and quantify both enantiomers together. Compare the real transferred mixture with metal-free and sulfur(IV)-free controls. A proposed screening gate is less than 20% reduction in aminonitrile recovery attributable to upstream carryover; this is an acceptance criterion, not an observed result."}, "mechanism": "HOCH2CH=NH + HCN -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CN. Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon, establishing the serine carbon skeleton and producing a racemic center without a chiral influence.", "reasoning": "The transformation is recognizable Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), consistent with cyanosulfidic access to amino-acid precursors (Patel et al., 2015). It identifies the correct serine aminonitrile rather than an isomeric amino alcohol or beta-amino nitrile. Productive formation does not establish successful amino-acid release; rxn_012 and rxn_013 remain separate completion bottlenecks."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Glycolaldehyde capture as glyceronitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed order-of-addition screen: glycolaldehyde 1-20 mM; total cyanide 5-50 mM; pH 7.5-8.5; 1-24 h before substantial ammonia addition. Quantify the equilibrium cyanohydrin pool and residual aldehyde. Use a shaded compartment to avoid unintended photoreduction of the new nitrile."}, "mechanism": "HOCH2CHO + HCN -> HOCH2CH(OH)CN. Reversible cyanide addition generates glyceronitrile, the glycolaldehyde cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "Glycolaldehyde cyanohydrin formation is chemically well founded within reductive-homologation chemistry (Ritson and Sutherland, 2012; Patel et al., 2015). This reaction stores the carbon skeleton but does not install the amino group. It is included only to examine a different order of reagent arrival, not as evidence for an independent serine-forming mechanism."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Ammonia-dependent transfer from the cyanohydrin reservoir to serine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed, unvalidated substrate-specific screen: glyceronitrile 1-20 mM; total ammonia/ammonium 0.2-1.0 M; pH 8.8-9.5; 12-168 h in darkness. Cyanide released during cyanohydrin dissociation supplies the trapping pool. Measure glyceronitrile disappearance, free aldehyde, aminonitrile appearance, and irreversible carbon loss. Require positive net aminonitrile production rather than merely rapid cyanohydrin exchange."}, "mechanism": "Net equation: HOCH2CH(OH)CN + NH3 -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CN + H2O. Mechanistically, the cyanohydrin dissociates to glycolaldehyde and HCN; glycolaldehyde forms a transient imine, which is trapped by cyanide. This is not direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Strecker chemistry supports the shared imine/cyanide mechanism (Strecker, 1850), but useful net flux from this particular cyanohydrin reservoir under the proposed natural conditions is not established by the cited work. This is an explicitly speculative order-of-addition branch, not a demonstrated shortcut. It is unnecessary for P1, P3, P5, P7, and P9. Failure of this equilibration branch eliminates the other five pathway entries without invalidating imine-first assembly."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Acid-catalyzed hydration of serine aminonitrile to serinamide", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions maintained by anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing condensate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Aqueous acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed environmental-window test: aminonitrile 0.1-10 mM; measured pH 0-1; nominal HCl 0.1-1.0 M before matrix-dependent corrections; 24-336 h in a shaded hot pool. Determine acid consumption by ammonia and minerals instead of assuming the initial pH persists. Measure aminonitrile, serinamide, serine, and degradation products at multiple times. These conditions are not claimed to be an established serine-aminonitrile operating window."}, "mechanism": "HOCH2CH(NH2)CN + H2O -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CONH2. Protonation activates the nitrile toward water addition, followed by imidic-acid/amide tautomerization. The equation uses neutral organic reference species; the amino group is predominantly protonated in the reaction pool.", "reasoning": "Acidic conversion of Strecker nitriles to amino acids has classical precedent (Strecker, 1850), but that precedent does not supply substrate-specific hydrolysis and survival rates for serine aminonitrile. This natural implementation is therefore an explicitly unvalidated extrapolation. Volcanic HCl supplies acidity without O2; sufficiently acidic, weakly buffered pools are a restrictive geochemical requirement, not a generic surface condition. A hydrothermal label or an elevated temperature alone would not resolve the kinetic bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Serinamide hydrolysis and retention of DL-serine", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions maintained by anoxic volcanic HCl-bearing condensate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "353-370 K during hydrolysis; below 310 K after downstream cooling", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Aqueous acid", "aqueous": true, "other": "Test together with rxn_012 at pH 0-1 for 24-336 h; serinamide is an explicit kinetic intermediate, not an assumed isolated transfer product. Use serine-spike controls to measure destruction in the actual matrix. Fit hydrolysis rates k12 and k13 and serine-loss rate k_loss. A conservative screening condition is 2.3/k12 + 2.3/k13 < 0.223/k_loss; independently require at least 50% molar serine recovery from the initial aminonitrile. Both are proposed acceptance criteria. Natural outflow into cooler water must be included in the recovery balance."}, "mechanism": "HOCH2CH(NH2)CONH2 + H2O -> HOCH2CH(NH2)CO2H + NH3. Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis releases serine and ammonia; ammonia is predominantly ammonium under the reaction conditions. Cooling and subsequent buffering change protonation, not the serine carbon skeleton.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is chemically established, but a substrate-specific natural production-versus-destruction window is not established here. Conventional amino-acid analysis often uses approximately 6 M HCl at 383 K for 24 h and documents residue-dependent losses, including serine; this is an analytical benchmark, not evidence that the proposed natural pool works (Fountoulakis and Lahm, 1998). The environmental implementation remains an unvalidated extrapolation. If measurable serine release requires only the harsher analytical treatment, the environmental synthesis claim must be rejected rather than substituting laboratory workup for prebiotic production."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "HCN-fed sulfide photochemistry with imine-first Strecker assembly", "description": "HCN supplies both the photoreduced carbon and homologating cyanide. Sulfide/copper photochemistry generates formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde, followed by transient-imine capture and the common conditional hydrolysis endpoint. This is a candidate integrated route, not a demonstrated natural sequence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "HCN-fed sulfide photochemistry with cyanohydrin-first assembly", "description": "Uses the same sulfide photochemical entry as P1, but glycolaldehyde encounters cyanide before concentrated ammonia. Productive transfer from glyceronitrile to aminonitrile is an additional unvalidated requirement. Its elementary mechanism still includes an imine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_016", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Formaldehyde-fed sulfide homologation with imine-first assembly", "description": "Begins with externally supplied formaldehyde, avoiding HCN-to-formaldehyde photoreduction. Glycolonitrile homologation supplies glycolaldehyde for imine-first Strecker chemistry. Feedstock availability, photochemical carryover, and the shared hydrolysis window remain conditional.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Formaldehyde-fed sulfide homologation with cyanohydrin-first assembly", "description": "Externally supplied formaldehyde enters sulfide-driven homologation, followed by glyceronitrile capture and ammonia-dependent redistribution. This is the order-of-addition counterpart of P3 and carries the extra rxn_011 flux uncertainty.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_016", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "HCN-fed sulfite photochemistry with imine-first assembly", "description": "Two sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemical stages produce explicit formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde bisulfite reservoirs. Both proposed recovery operations must release useful carbonyl flux before imine-first Strecker assembly. No copper/sulfide stage is mixed into this pathway.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "HCN-fed sulfite photochemistry with cyanohydrin-first assembly", "description": "Combines the two bisulfite-reservoir recovery requirements of P5 with the additional cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile flux requirement. It is more condition-dependent than its imine-first counterpart and is not assigned an evidentiary bonus for its additional steps.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_016", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Formaldehyde-fed sulfite homologation with imine-first assembly", "description": "Externally supplied formaldehyde avoids the first sulfite photoreduction and formaldehyde-release operation. Glycolonitrile is homologated into the glycolaldehyde bisulfite reservoir, whose measured release must support imine-first Strecker chemistry.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Formaldehyde-fed sulfite homologation with cyanohydrin-first assembly", "description": "Uses the same supplied-formaldehyde entry as P7, followed by glycolaldehyde recovery, cyanohydrin capture, and ammonia-dependent aminonitrile formation. Both residual sulfur(IV) effects and cyanohydrin exchange must be tested quantitatively.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_013", "mol_008", "mol_016", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Supplied glycolaldehyde with imine-first Strecker assembly", "description": "Begins with externally supplied glycolaldehyde, ammonia, and cyanide. This is the least assumption-intensive target-forming subnetwork under that feedstock boundary condition, because it avoids photoredox speciation, bisulfite recovery, and cyanohydrin-reservoir conversion. Its serine-producing environmental hydrolysis window nevertheless remains unvalidated.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Supplied glycolaldehyde with cyanohydrin-first assembly", "description": "Externally supplied glycolaldehyde first encounters cyanide, then ammonia. This tests whether a stored glyceronitrile pool can feed the same imine-dependent Strecker endpoint. It is sequence-distinct from P9 but is not an independent elementary reaction mechanism.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_015"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_005", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_002"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_007", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_006"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_007"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_008", "rxn_010"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_015", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_011"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_012"]}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 462997.7023601532, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T10:39:36.376936", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Serine (racemic DL-serine)\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct serine's carbon skeleton by glycolaldehyde Strecker chemistry, followed by explicitly resolved nitrile-to-amide and amide-to-acid hydrolysis. Five entry options are combined with two Strecker order-of-addition options to provide ten sequence-distinct pathways. These are overlapping process alternatives, not ten independently demonstrated mechanisms: both Strecker options ultimately require imine formation and cyanide capture. Formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde, ammonia, and cyanide may be externally supplied where stated. Their supply is a boundary condition, not an implied synthesis reaction. Sulfide/copper and sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry are kept in separate pathways; compatibility of mixed photochemical streams is not assumed. Numerical conditions below are proposed experimental screening ranges unless explicitly identified as established analytical practice. In particular, a naturally accessible, serine-preserving hydrolysis window remains unvalidated; no literature-supported numerical confidence of 0.90 is claimed. P9 requires the fewest unvalidated upstream operations when glycolaldehyde is supplied. All stages use anoxic surface settings, including shaded geothermal pools. Balanced equations are included in mechanism fields without repeating molecule IDs. Organic formulas use neutral reference structures; actual protonation follows the stated pH. Spectator counterions are omitted. Terminal coproducts use the schema's intermediate role because no coproduct role is available.\n\nMOLECULES (20 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_004: S8 (Elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_005: CH2O (Formaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: H3N (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_007: C2H3NO (Glycolonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: C2H4O2 (Glycolaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_009: O3S-2 (Sulfite ion) [starting]\n  - mol_010: HO3S- (Bisulfite ion) [starting]\n  - mol_011: O4S-2 (Sulfate ion) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: CH3O4S- (Formaldehyde bisulfite adduct) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C2H5O5S- (Glycolaldehyde bisulfite adduct) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C2H5NO (Glycolaldehyde imine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C3H6N2O (Serine aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C3H5NO2 (Glyceronitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C3H8N2O2 (Serinamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C3H7NO3 (DL-Serine) [target]\n  - mol_019: H+ (Hydrogen ion) [starting]\n  - mol_020: O2S (Sulfur dioxide) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (13 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_005, mol_006, mol_004 (Sulfide-driven photoreduction of hydrogen cyanide to formaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_005, mol_001 -> mol_007 (Formaldehyde capture as glycolonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_007, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_008, mol_006, mol_004 (Sulfide-driven photoreductive homologation of glycolonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_001, mol_002, mol_009, mol_010 -> mol_012, mol_006, mol_011 (Sulfite/ferrocyanide photoreduction of HCN with formaldehyde trapping) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_012, mol_019 -> mol_005, mol_020, mol_002 (Acid-assist